            <ArticleTitle>Impact of traffic flow on the asthma prevalence among school children in Lima, Peru.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>197-202</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To estimate the impact of traffic flow on the prevalence of asthma among school children of 6 to 7 and 13 to 14-years of age.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional study consisting of 5,917 children selected in schools of the Cone Norte of Lima, Peru.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">For the 6- to 7-year age group the prevalence of medical diagnosis of asthma according to the traffic flow index was 8.6% (CI: 6.8-10.8), 10.3% (CI: 8.4-12.4), and 15.3% (CI:13.3-17.5) at low, medium and high, respectively, and for the 13- to 14-year age group, 11.9% (CI: 9.4-14.6), 13.3% (CI: 11.6-15.2), and 17.1% (CI: 14.7-19.6).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">For both groups, the prevalence of asthma was significantly related to traffic flow density.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
